1
00:00:00,593 --> 00:00:03,434
Now, I've mentioned it before in this course

2
00:00:03,964 --> 00:00:06,259
that besides using these proprietary models

3
00:00:06,371 --> 00:00:08,024
by OpenAI, Google, and so on,

4
00:00:08,682 --> 00:00:10,191
you can also run

5
00:00:10,929 --> 00:00:12,598
open large language models

6
00:00:13,192 --> 00:00:15,728
locally on your system or on some server

7
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:16,434
owned by you.

8
00:00:17,124 --> 00:00:19,355
Now, it depends on your hardware, but for

9
00:00:19,355 --> 00:00:20,029
example, on

10
00:00:20,574 --> 00:00:23,511
relatively modern MacBooks, you can run many

11
00:00:23,543 --> 00:00:25,839
of these models without problems and also on

12
00:00:26,176 --> 00:00:27,603
many of other devices.

13
00:00:28,421 --> 00:00:29,832
As also mentioned before,

14
00:00:30,361 --> 00:00:32,703
you can use a tool called Ollama

15
00:00:33,232 --> 00:00:35,301
for this, and attached you find a link to

16
00:00:35,333 --> 00:00:38,219
that. And this tool is available for free

17
00:00:38,524 --> 00:00:40,865
for all major operating systems. You can

18
00:00:40,865 --> 00:00:43,736
download it from their website, ollama.com.

19
00:00:44,554 --> 00:00:47,008
Once you did install it, you can run the

20
00:00:47,104 --> 00:00:50,359
Ollama program from inside your terminal or

21
00:00:50,391 --> 00:00:51,017
command line.

22
00:00:51,690 --> 00:00:53,551
And when you do run this program, you get

23
00:00:53,615 --> 00:00:57,191
various commands you can execute to download

24
00:00:57,447 --> 00:01:00,108
and run models on your system.

25
00:01:00,910 --> 00:01:03,090
And you can visit the Ollama model catalog

26
00:01:03,138 --> 00:01:04,388
to get an idea for

27
00:01:04,981 --> 00:01:07,402
which models you can use. For example, the

28
00:01:07,402 --> 00:01:10,656
Gemma 3 models, which are Google's open

29
00:01:10,912 --> 00:01:13,605
models. So Google does not just have its

30
00:01:13,637 --> 00:01:16,298
paid Gemini models, which you can access

31
00:01:16,362 --> 00:01:18,623
through their API, but they also have the

32
00:01:18,703 --> 00:01:21,268
Gemma models, which you can use for free

33
00:01:21,588 --> 00:01:23,207
with Ollama, for example.

34
00:01:24,217 --> 00:01:26,319
Though it's worth noting that all these open

35
00:01:26,319 --> 00:01:28,789
models typically also come with a license,

36
00:01:28,805 --> 00:01:30,729
which you should Google and check before you

37
00:01:30,729 --> 00:01:33,231
start using it to find out if you, for

38
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:35,140
example, can use the model commercially.

39
00:01:36,006 --> 00:01:38,669
Many are allowed to be used commercially,

40
00:01:38,749 --> 00:01:40,561
but some are more restricted.

41
00:01:41,155 --> 00:01:43,368
The Gemma 3 models are quite permissive

42
00:01:43,545 --> 00:01:43,705
though.

43
00:01:45,068 --> 00:01:47,827
Now, the Gemma 3 model here comes in various

44
00:01:47,827 --> 00:01:50,650
sizes and even has vision support, so it can

45
00:01:50,714 --> 00:01:52,880
even analyze images. As a side note.

46
00:01:53,892 --> 00:01:55,931
And which size you wanna use simply depends

47
00:01:55,979 --> 00:01:58,147
on your hardware and the task you wanna

48
00:01:58,147 --> 00:01:58,548
perform.

49
00:01:59,190 --> 00:02:01,904
The bigger, the better, but the more memory

50
00:02:02,113 --> 00:02:04,313
is required. I mentioned before that you can

51
00:02:04,345 --> 00:02:06,833
roughly take this number by 2 to get the

52
00:02:06,833 --> 00:02:09,402
amount of memory you need on your system to

53
00:02:09,467 --> 00:02:10,703
load and run this model.

54
00:02:11,426 --> 00:02:13,963
So a 4 billion parameter model would need

55
00:02:13,963 --> 00:02:16,419
around 8 gigabytes of memory plus some

56
00:02:16,612 --> 00:02:16,917
overhead.

57
00:02:17,817 --> 00:02:20,522
So for example, here I can run ollama pull

58
00:02:20,731 --> 00:02:24,532
gemma-3-4b-itqat

59
00:02:25,063 --> 00:02:26,931
to download this specific version of the

60
00:02:26,980 --> 00:02:27,900
Gemma 3 model.

61
00:02:29,111 --> 00:02:30,678
Now, how do I know this identifier?

62
00:02:31,534 --> 00:02:34,086
Well, on this Gemma 3 page, you can either

63
00:02:34,118 --> 00:02:35,766
use the identifiers you see here,

64
00:02:37,496 --> 00:02:40,003
or if you scroll down a bit, you will see

65
00:02:40,003 --> 00:02:42,331
that in case of Gemma, there are these

66
00:02:42,364 --> 00:02:44,676
quantization-aware trained models, which

67
00:02:44,789 --> 00:02:46,883
don't exist for every open model, but which

68
00:02:46,997 --> 00:02:47,538
do exist for the Gemma models.

69
00:02:48,712 --> 00:02:50,591
Which are simply models which are even more

70
00:02:50,784 --> 00:02:53,161
optimized to run on consumer hardware.

71
00:02:53,434 --> 00:02:55,699
Again, I dive a bit deeper into all of that

72
00:02:55,699 --> 00:02:58,398
in my dedicated Open Large Language Model

73
00:02:58,430 --> 00:02:58,719
course.

74
00:02:59,972 --> 00:03:02,012
So in the end, that's the command I ran

75
00:03:02,044 --> 00:03:05,578
here, just not run but pull to not start a

76
00:03:05,578 --> 00:03:07,810
chat session with that model, but instead to

77
00:03:07,810 --> 00:03:08,726
just download it.

78
00:03:09,336 --> 00:03:11,569
And you need to download it in order to use

79
00:03:11,585 --> 00:03:12,324
it on your system.

80
00:03:13,127 --> 00:03:15,117
Now this can take a while. I did already

81
00:03:15,133 --> 00:03:17,232
download it, which is why it's quite fast.

82
00:03:18,338 --> 00:03:20,132
And once you did download it, you actually

83
00:03:20,325 --> 00:03:21,671
don't need to do anything else.

84
00:03:22,248 --> 00:03:24,138
You just need to make sure that you ran

85
00:03:24,331 --> 00:03:26,430
Ollama at least once so that the Ollama

86
00:03:26,430 --> 00:03:28,000
process is up and running.

87
00:03:28,753 --> 00:03:31,285
Because when that process is up and running,

88
00:03:31,702 --> 00:03:35,131
Ollama also spins up a server that runs on

89
00:03:35,243 --> 00:03:37,855
your system, so on your machine, which is

90
00:03:37,983 --> 00:03:40,563
not available to anyone else but only to you

91
00:03:40,771 --> 00:03:42,055
on on your computer.

92
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:46,535
And this server exposes an API which you can

93
00:03:46,631 --> 00:03:50,228
use to communicate with an open model that's

94
00:03:50,244 --> 00:03:53,263
running on your machine through Ollama on

95
00:03:53,359 --> 00:03:55,222
your system programmatically.

96
00:03:56,667 --> 00:03:59,140
So you can then send, for example, POST

97
00:03:59,140 --> 00:04:00,761
requests to this endpoint

98
00:04:01,307 --> 00:04:03,475
and configure the request to use this

99
00:04:03,475 --> 00:04:04,470
locally running model

100
00:04:05,177 --> 00:04:06,220
for whatever you wanna do.

101
00:04:06,932 --> 00:04:08,232
And that's what I did here.

102
00:04:08,817 --> 00:04:11,368
Attached, you find this open.py file,

103
00:04:12,110 --> 00:04:14,417
which is very similar to the main.py file

104
00:04:14,417 --> 00:04:17,225
from before. It has the same multi-step,

105
00:04:17,709 --> 00:04:19,839
multi-agentic workflow,

106
00:04:20,694 --> 00:04:23,622
but which is now not using OpenAI in any

107
00:04:23,622 --> 00:04:27,652
step, but instead only Ollama or the Gemma

108
00:04:27,652 --> 00:04:28,802
models specifically.

109
00:04:30,218 --> 00:04:33,819
So here in the get_ai_response function,

110
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:36,290
which I added for this open version here.

111
00:04:36,899 --> 00:04:39,304
I'm sending a request to my local machine

112
00:04:39,304 --> 00:04:41,484
and then to API generate, which is this

113
00:04:41,548 --> 00:04:43,712
endpoint exposed by the Ollama server.

114
00:04:45,075 --> 00:04:47,031
I then provide some information about the

115
00:04:47,031 --> 00:04:49,804
